---
import { FAQSection, RelatedArticles } from "../../components/article";
import ArticleLayout from "../../layouts/ArticleLayout.astro";
import { getArticleBySlug } from "../../data/articles";

// Get article metadata from central data file
const article = getArticleBySlug("minimalist-home-office-setup", "guides")!;
const { title, description, authorSlug, readingTime, image, imageAlt } = article;
const pubDate = new Date(article.pubDate);

const headings = [
  { depth: 2, slug: "introduction", text: "Why Less Stuff Actually Works" },
  { depth: 2, slug: "core-principles", text: "The Principles That Actually Work" },
  { depth: 2, slug: "essential-items", text: "What You Actually Need" },
  { depth: 2, slug: "declutter-process", text: "How to Actually Declutter" },
  { depth: 2, slug: "desk-setup", text: "The Setup Itself" },
  { depth: 2, slug: "digital-minimalism", text: "Digital Clutter Counts" },
  { depth: 2, slug: "maintaining-minimalism", text: "Keeping It This Way" },
  { depth: 2, slug: "faq", text: "Questions People Ask" },
];

const faqData = [
  {
    question: "Isn't minimalism about suffering?",
    answer:
      "No. It's about keeping what adds value. Removing what doesn't. Some minimalist setups have quite a few things. Every item just has a reason to be there.",
  },
  {
    question: "Can I work productively with just a laptop?",
    answer:
      "Many people do. If you're working eight hour days, external monitor and keyboard help ergonomically. Even minimalists prioritize not destroying their necks.",
  },
  {
    question: "What about paperwork?",
    answer:
      "Scan it. Cloud storage. For things you absolutely must keep physically, one small folder. Not a cabinet.",
  },
  {
    question: "Can I keep personal items?",
    answer:
      "One or two meaningful things is fine. A plant. A photo. Something that makes you feel good. Minimalism isn't sterile. It's intentional.",
  },
  {
    question: "Standing desk?",
    answer:
      "Actually fits minimalist thinking. One piece of furniture that does two jobs. Manual converters are even simpler since they work with your existing desk.",
  },
  {
    question: "Family thinks my workspace looks empty?",
    answer:
      "Explain that it helps you work better. Better work means you finish faster. Finishing faster means more present time with them. Usually wins people over.",
  },
];

const relatedArticles = [
  {
    title: "Complete Home Office Checklist",
    excerpt: "Full checklist of everything you might need.",
    href: "/guides/complete-home-office-checklist",
    image: "/images/unsplash/photo-1518455027359-f3f8164ba6bd-800w.webp",
    imageAlt: "Well-organized home office setup",
  },
  {
    title: "Cable Management Guide",
    excerpt: "Keep your workspace clean and organized.",
    href: "/guides/cable-management-guide",
    image: "/images/unsplash/photo-1558618666-fcd25c85cd64-800w.webp",
    imageAlt: "Organized cables under desk",
  },
  {
    title: "Best Standing Desks",
    excerpt: "Top standing desks for health and productivity.",
    href: "/gear/best-standing-desks",
    image: "/images/unsplash/photo-1611269154421-4e27233ac5c7-800w.webp",
    imageAlt: "Standing desk in home office",
  },
];
---

<ArticleLayout
    title={title}
    description={description}
    authorSlug={authorSlug}
    pubDate={pubDate}
    readingTime={readingTime}
    image={image}
    imageAlt={imageAlt}
    category="Setup"
    categorySlug="guides"
    subcategorySlug="setup"
    headings={headings}
>
  <p class="lead">
    Realized last year my desk had become a disaster. Four pens, old receipts, random charging cables, a stack of books
    I kept meaning to read. My brain felt cluttered too. Every morning felt like I had to push through the junk just to
    start working.
  </p>

  <p>
    Started over. Stripped everything off the desk. Gave away half of it. Kept what I actually used.
  </p>

  <p>
    Best decision I made for my work-from-home life.
  </p>

  <h2 id="introduction">Why Less Stuff Actually Works</h2>

  <p>
    Your brain processes every item in your visual field. Even stuff you're ignoring. Subconsciously your mind is
    tracking that old coffee mug, those scattered papers, that charger you haven't used in months. Mental energy gets
    wasted.
  </p>

  <p>
    Remote work makes this worse. Already fighting distractions from home life. Kids, laundry, that weird noise the
    fridge makes. Adding desk clutter to the mix? Recipe for scattered thinking.
  </p>

  <p>
    What actually changes when you strip things down:
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>You focus faster. Less visual noise competing for attention.</li>
    <li>Decisions get simpler. Less stuff to manage, organize, lose.</li>
    <li>Morning startup takes seconds. Sit down, open laptop, work.</li>
    <li>Money stays in your pocket. Stop buying things that don't help.</li>
    <li>Cleaning becomes trivial. Dust a flat surface? Takes thirty seconds.</li>
    <li>You can work anywhere. Pack up and move in five minutes.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="core-principles">The Principles That Actually Work</h2>

  <h3>Everything Needs a Reason</h3>
  <p>
    Random stuff accumulates. It just does. Fighting that means questioning everything on your desk. What does this do
    for me? When did I last use it? Would I miss it?
  </p>
  <p>
    Kept a small cactus my daughter gave me. Gets water once a month, makes me smile. That earns its spot. The stress
    ball from some conference three years ago? Gone.
  </p>

  <h3>One Good Thing Beats Three Mediocre Things</h3>
  <p>
    Had three different notepads at one point. Why? Grabbed whichever was closest. Now I have one decent notebook. When
    it's full, I buy another one. Same pen. Same water bottle. Same everything.
  </p>
  <p>
    Weird how much mental energy gets freed up when you stop managing multiples.
  </p>

  <h3>Clear Surfaces When Not Working</h3>
  <p>
    During work: laptop, coffee, maybe headphones. End of day: everything goes away. Desk becomes empty. That emptiness
    signals to my brain that work is done. Important boundary when home and office are the same building.
  </p>

  <h3>Buy Less But Buy Better</h3>
  <p>
    This is counterintuitive. Minimalism isn't about being cheap. My <a href="/gear/best-ergonomic-chairs">chair</a>
    cost real money. But I don't have a chair
    plus a seat cushion plus a lumbar pillow plus a footrest. Just one thing that works.
  </p>

  <h3>Less Paper, More Pixels</h3>
  <p>
    Notes in an app. Documents in cloud storage. The only paper on my desk is whatever I'm actively working with.
    Usually nothing.
  </p>

  <h2 id="essential-items">What You Actually Need</h2>

  <p>
    The bare minimum to do knowledge work:
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>Some kind of surface. Table, desk, kitchen counter. Doesn't matter.</li>
    <li>Something to sit on. Chair helps.</li>
    <li>A computer. Obviously.</li>
    <li>Internet that works. WiFi or plugged in.</li>
    <li>Light. Preferably from a window.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    That's it. Five things. Everything else is optional.
  </p>

  <h3>For People Doing This Full-Time</h3>
  <p>Eight plus hours:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>External <a href="/gear/best-monitors-remote-work">monitor</a>. Your neck will thank you.</li>
    <li>Real <a href="/gear/best-mechanical-keyboards">keyboard</a>. Laptop keyboards are tolerable, not great.</li>
    <li>Mouse. Trackpads work but mice are better for extended use.</li>
    <li><a href="/gear/best-noise-canceling-headphones">Headphones</a>. For calls and for blocking out the world.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Maybe Nice But Not Required</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Better <a href="/gear/best-webcams-video-calls">webcam</a> if you're on video calls constantly.</li>
    <li>One plant. Not five. One.</li>
    <li>Small notebook for quick scribbles during meetings.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>What You Don't Need</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Multiple monitors. One big one is enough for most work.</li>
    <li>Those drawer organizers full of clips and rubber bands.</li>
    <li>A printer. Seriously. Go to Staples twice a year.</li>
    <li>Filing cabinets. Everything is digital now.</li>
    <li>Decorations you feel obligated to keep.</li>
    <li>That gadget you bought and used twice.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="declutter-process">How to Actually Declutter</h2>

  <p>
    Did this on a Sunday afternoon. Took about two hours.
  </p>

  <p>
    Clear everything off the desk. Everything. Box it up or put it on the floor. Look at the empty surface. Notice how
    peaceful that feels.
  </p>

  <p>
    Clean it. Wipe it down. Start fresh.
  </p>

  <p>
    Add things back one at a time. Only if you genuinely need them to do work tomorrow. Not "might need someday." Need
    tomorrow.
  </p>

  <p>
    Everything else needs a home that isn't your desk. Drawer. Closet. Donation box. Garbage. Be honest with yourself
    about what you actually use.
  </p>

  <p>
    Going forward: one thing comes in, one thing goes out. Prevents the pile-up from happening again.
  </p>

  <h2 id="desk-setup">The Setup Itself</h2>

  <p>
    During work hours my desk has:
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>Monitor at eye level. Or laptop on a stand.</li>
    <li>Keyboard and mouse in front.</li>
    <li>One item off to the side. Usually coffee.</li>
    <li>That's all.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    Everything else is hidden. In drawers, in a cabinet, somewhere not visible.
  </p>

  <h3>End of Day Ritual</h3>
  <p>
    Two minutes. Non-negotiable. Before I close the laptop:
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>Put away whatever accumulated during the day.</li>
    <li>Any paper gets dealt with or trashed.</li>
    <li>Quick wipe down.</li>
    <li>Tomorrow morning's desk is ready now.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    This creates a real ending to the workday. Important when your commute is twelve steps down the hall.
  </p>

  <h3>Cables Are Visual Clutter Too</h3>
  <p>
    Wireless everything that can be wireless. Keyboard, mouse, headphones.
  </p>

  <p>
    One cable to the laptop if using a dock setup. Everything else hidden behind the desk or under it.
  </p>

  <p>
    Power strips go underneath where you can't see them.
  </p>

  <h2 id="digital-minimalism">Digital Clutter Counts</h2>

  <p>
    Clean desk with chaotic computer defeats the purpose.
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>Desktop should be empty or nearly empty. Files go in folders.</li>
    <li>Browser tabs get closed. Bookmark things for later instead of leaving forty tabs open.</li>
    <li>Notifications off except truly urgent stuff. Check messages on your schedule.</li>
    <li>One tool per job. Not three note apps. One.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="maintaining-minimalism">Keeping It This Way</h2>

  <p>
    Daily: Start clean, end clean.
  </p>

  <p>
    Weekly: Quick scan. What accumulated? Get it off the desk.
  </p>

  <p>
    Monthly: Full review. Does everything still deserve to be here?
  </p>

  <h3>The Hard Parts</h3>

  <p>
    Things pile up when you're busy. Fix: Friday afternoon reset. Fifteen minutes. Put it on the calendar.
  </p>

  <p>
    Need reference materials for work. Fix: Digital where possible. Physical stuff goes in a closed cabinet, not
    visible.
  </p>

  <p>
    Family keeps leaving stuff in your workspace. Fix: Small basket. Things that appear get moved to the basket. Basket
    gets emptied elsewhere daily.
  </p>

  <h2 id="faq">Questions People Ask</h2>

  <FAQSection faqs={faqData}/>

  <h2>Where to Start</h2>

  <p>
    Don't try to achieve perfect minimalism by Friday. Take one thing off your desk today. Something you don't use,
    don't need, don't love.
  </p>

  <p>
    Tomorrow, another thing.
  </p>

  <p>
    Week from now, your desk has only what matters.
  </p>

  <p>
    The goal isn't owning the least stuff. It's having exactly what you need. Peaceful space. Clear mind. Ready to work.
  </p>

  <p>
    If your current setup doesn't feel like that, maybe time to strip some things away.
  </p>

  <Fragment slot="full-width">
    <RelatedArticles articles={relatedArticles}/>
  </Fragment>
</ArticleLayout>
